  "noMusicReason": "Standing rule (2026-08-24 user directive): reels ship voice + SFX only, no music bed by default. Set plan[\"music\"] = true (or a full music object) to opt this reel back into a bed.",
  "format": "news",
  "showCredits": false,
  "script": "You can now pull someone into a WhatsApp call who doesn't even have WhatsApp.\n\nWhatsApp just launched it as guest calling. No account, no cost, no time limit.\n\nBut the catch sits on the guest's side, and it decides who this really helps. Hold on to that.\n\nTo set one up, open the Calls tab, tap the plus, and create a call link. Then send it to anyone.\n\nWhen they open it, they type a name and wait. Nobody gets in until you let them. You can also remove a guest at any point.\n\nOnce inside, a guest can share their screen, raise a hand, or react. But they can't see anyone's phone number, send messages, or add more people.\n\nAnd the call itself stays end-to-end encrypted, with room for up to 32 people.\n\nAbout that catch. WhatsApp's own steps say the guest opens the link on a computer. Not on their phone. That suits the client on a work laptop, or the relative at a desk who will never install the app.\n\nLater this year, WhatsApp says these links will plug into Google Calendar and Outlook invites too.\n\nWho's the first person you'd send one to?",
